---
// Canonical copy: docs/builds/ai-explainer-producer.md (edit there, then regenerate this body).
// Static page on purpose — see the note in src content config (a content collection here
// busted the build cache and blew Vercel's 45-minute Hobby build timeout).
// Stills are real frames from the ENSv2 production master (src/assets/building/).
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import brodyFrame from '../../assets/building/ai-explainer-producer-brody.jpg';
import statusFrame from '../../assets/building/ai-explainer-producer-status-board.jpg';

const title = 'I Built an AI Agent That Makes Explainer Videos';
const status = 'SHIPPED';
const date = 'October 2026';
const summary =
  'A reusable HyperAgent production agent that researches a technical topic, verifies it against primary sources, scripts it, and assembles an explainer with an AI presenter. I tested it on a real ENSv2 video, published the result, and kept track of the human work it still needed.';
const stack = ['AI Agents', 'HyperAgent', 'HeyGen', 'Video Production'];
const videoId = 'L8LzPXzMbQg';
const videoUrl = `https://youtu.be/${videoId}`;
const videoTitle = 'ENSv2 Explained: New Prices, Migration & What .eth Owners Need to Know';
const pipeline = ['Research', 'Verify claims', 'Script', 'Storyboard', 'Cast presenter', 'Pilot', 'Produce', 'Assemble & QA', 'Human edit'];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description="How I built a reusable AI video-production agent with HyperAgent, HeyGen, Claude, and FFmpeg, then tested it by creating an ENSv2 explainer."
  image="/og/ai-explainer-producer.jpg"
  ogImageWidth={1200}
  ogImageHeight={675}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Building', path: '/building/' },
    { name: title, path: '/building/ai-explainer-producer/' },
  ]}
>
  <article class="build">
    <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/building/"><span aria-hidden="true">&larr;</span> Building</a>
    </nav>

    <header class="build-header">
      <p class="page-mark" aria-hidden="true">// build</p>
      <div class="title-row">
        <h1>{title}</h1>
        <span class={`badge badge-${status.toLowerCase()}`}>
          <span class="badge-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          {status}
        </span>
      </div>
      <p class="summary">{summary}</p>
      <p class="meta">
        <span class="meta-key">{status === 'LIVE' ? 'Live since' : 'Shipped'}</span>
        {date}
      </p>
      <ul class="stack" aria-label="Stack">
        {stack.map((s) => <li>{s}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <figure class="video" id="video">
        <div class="video-frame">
          <iframe
            src={`https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/${videoId}?rel=0`}
            title={`Ken Ashe: ${videoTitle}`}
            width="560"
            height="315"
            loading="lazy"
            allow="accelerometer; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share"
            allowfullscreen
            referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
          ></iframe>
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          The finished ENSv2 explainer (3:56): my own opening and closing, plus the AI presenter and visuals the agent produced.
          <a href={videoUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube ↗</a>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>The Problem</h2>
      <p>I wanted to understand the proposed ENSv2 changes. Instead of reading every proposal, forum post and documentation page myself, I used AI to help research it. That turned into a bigger question: could I build an agent that takes a complicated topic and produces an accurate, watchable YouTube explainer with relatively little manual production work?</p>
      <p>A good technical explainer takes a lot of different skills: research, fact-checking, scriptwriting, presenting, graphics, editing, captions and formatting for each platform. I wanted to see how much of that a reusable production system could carry.</p>

      <h2>What I Built</h2>
      <p>AI Explainer Producer is a named agent in HyperAgent. It is not a one-off prompt. It runs on two reusable skills: a stage-gated production method (verify claims, script, storyboard, cast, pilot, produce, check) and a Python assembly toolkit that stitches presenter takes, graphics, captions and audio into finished videos.</p>
      <ol class="pipeline" aria-label="Production pipeline">
        {pipeline.map((step) => <li>{step}</li>)}
      </ol>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>HyperAgent</strong> runs the agent and its tools.</li>
        <li><strong>Claude Opus 5.5</strong> handles production planning and editorial decisions; <strong>Sonnet subagents</strong> take supporting research and production tasks.</li>
        <li><strong>HeyGen Avatar V</strong> generates the virtual presenter, Brody, a stock HeyGen avatar.</li>
        <li><strong>HyperFrames and GSAP</strong> render the diagrams and animated explanations.</li>
        <li><strong>Python and FFmpeg</strong> assemble the timeline and run technical checks: loudness, dead air, black or frozen frames.</li>
        <li><strong>CapCut</strong> is where I did the final human edit.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Every stage ends at a checkpoint. The agent doesn’t pick a presenter, start full production, or publish anything without my sign-off.</p>

      <h2>The First Experiment: ENSv2</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Start from a draft.</strong> I began with a six-page script Claude had written.</li>
        <li><strong>Verify it.</strong> The agent checked the claims against primary ENS sources (the activation proposal, the pricing proposal, and the official migration and registrar documentation) and kept a claim-by-claim ledger. Claims were verified, adjusted, or cut; only verified or carefully qualified ones made it into the video. That pass removed things the draft got wrong, including a line suggesting I had personally reviewed every contract call.</li>
        <li><strong>Rewrite for the audience.</strong> The seven-to-eight-minute draft became an owner-focused script of about 500 words built around four questions: what will I pay, do I have to migrate, when could I lose my name, and what changes?</li>
        <li><strong>Storyboard it.</strong> The storyboard used source-labeled diagrams and real screenshots of ENS documentation, with no generated interfaces or fake transactions.</li>
        <li><strong>Cast the presenter.</strong> The agent generated six short auditions from stock HeyGen presenters and voices. I chose Brody with his default voice.</li>
        <li><strong>Pilot first.</strong> A roughly 30-second pilot tested the presenter, a diagram, a real screenshot, captions, and both 16:9 and 9:16 cuts before committing to the full video.</li>
        <li><strong>Improve, then produce.</strong> The pilot led to better graphics, larger and more readable source lines, an on-screen AI disclosure, and no silent title cards. Then the agent produced the four presenter sections and assembled the master plus two vertical cuts.</li>
        <li><strong>Add the human parts.</strong> I filmed my own opening and closing on an iPhone, finished the edit and audio in CapCut, and published it.</li>
      </ol>
      <figure class="still">
        <Image src={brodyFrame} alt="Brody, the AI-generated presenter, in a living room with a record shelf, labeled on screen as an AI-generated presenter." widths={[640, 1280]} sizes="(min-width: 48rem) 720px, calc(100vw - 3rem)" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Brody, the AI presenter, with the on-screen disclosure. Frame from the production master.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>What Worked</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>A consistent presenter.</strong> The same face, voice and setting across every section. The four sections came out word-accurate with no retakes.</li>
        <li><strong>Fact-checking against primary sources.</strong> The ledger made every claim traceable to an official page.</li>
        <li><strong>Clear visuals for complicated ideas.</strong> Pricing, migration and grace-period rules became diagrams instead of paragraphs.</li>
        <li><strong>Reusable skills.</strong> The method and the assembly toolkit aren’t tied to ENS; the next topic starts from the same system.</li>
        <li><strong>Automated assembly and QA.</strong> Multiple segments, captions and loudness checks without hand-editing each piece.</li>
        <li><strong>Multiple formats.</strong> A horizontal master and two vertical clips for short-form platforms.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Most importantly, it produced a real, published video, not just a prototype.</p>

      <h2>What Didn’t Work Perfectly</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The facts moved mid-production.</strong> The ENS activation vote went live while the video was being made, so the agent rechecked the sources, re-recorded one presenter line, and rebuilt the status graphic from fresh screenshots.</li>
        <li><strong>Graphics needed fixing.</strong> Headlines with a stray rule through them, a timeline missing its bars, overlapping text on a vertical card, a font that rendered as boxes. All caught in review and re-rendered.</li>
        <li><strong>Captions and on-screen text needed corrections.</strong></li>
        <li><strong>An assembly-script bug</strong> broke builds without a music track, and had to be patched.</li>
        <li><strong>The first pilot</strong> exposed pacing problems and source lines too small to read.</li>
        <li><strong>I still recorded my own footage,</strong> and my audio needed noise reduction and volume adjustments in CapCut.</li>
        <li><strong>Final editorial judgment and the decision to publish</strong> were mine.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>This was not a fully autonomous production. The agent did most of the research, scripting, presenting, graphics and assembly; I directed it, checked it, appeared in it, and stood behind it.</p>
      <figure class="still">
        <Image src={statusFrame} alt="On-screen status board titled Where things actually stand, built from real Tally screenshots of the ENS activation proposal and live vote." widths={[640, 1280]} sizes="(min-width: 48rem) 720px, calc(100vw - 3rem)" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>The status board, rebuilt from real Tally captures after the vote went live. Frame from the production master.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>What I Learned</h2>
      <p>AI can do a substantial share of video production now. The human still supplies the direction, the judgment, the authenticity and the accountability.</p>
      <p>Building a reusable workflow was worth more than asking a tool to make one video. The checkpoints, the claim ledger and the assembly toolkit are what I’ll keep.</p>
      <p>And making content with AI can itself be content worth sharing. This page is part of that.</p>

      <h2>What’s Next</h2>
      <p>I plan to keep testing the agent on other technical subjects. Future experiments might include better virtual presenters, shorter production time, platform-specific edits, and measuring whether AI-assisted videos perform differently from ones I make the traditional way. None of that is done yet.</p>

      <h2>Related Builds</h2>
      <p>This builds on my <a href="/building/ai-ugc-video-lab/">AI UGC video lab</a>, where an agent generated short, fictional ad concepts with a consistent AI-generated creator. AI Explainer Producer takes the same idea further: from short experimental clips to a researched, source-verified educational video with a real person in it.</p>

      <p><a href={videoUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Watch the ENSv2 explainer on YouTube ↗</strong></a></p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
